## Is depression a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. Depression that lasts, or is likely to last, 12 months or more, or keeps coming back, can be covered.

## At work

Reasonable adjustments could include:

- flexible hours, or a later start if medicines make mornings hard
- time off for therapy and appointments
- a **phased return** after sick leave
- regular check-ins with your manager, and clear priorities
- a quieter place to work, or working from home

A **Wellness Action Plan**, from Mind, can help you and your manager agree what support you need.

## Access to Work

**Access to Work** offers free, confidential **Mental Health Support**, with up to 9 months of advice and a support plan to help you stay in or return to work.

## Benefits

PIP is based on how depression affects you on most days. It can affect activities such as:

- **preparing food** and **eating**, if you need prompting because of low motivation
- **washing** and **dressing**, if you need encouragement
- **managing medicines**, if you need reminders or someone to keep them safe
- **mixing with other people**, and **planning journeys**, if they cause overwhelming distress
- **making budgeting decisions**, if you need help with money

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

## If you cannot work

If your health means you cannot work, or can only work a little, you may be able to get [New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/) or extra money in [Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/). These are separate from PIP, and you can get them at the same time.

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
